 And, um, Rocky Mountain Power wouldn't let him do it, um, through the way that he wanted to do it.

01:55:56.580 --> 01:55:58.280
 So, there's, there's...

01:55:58.280 --> 01:56:02.220
 We need to find out if there's a way to be able to do it without everything running through Rocky Mountain.

01:56:02.220 --> 01:56:03.020
 Yeah.

01:56:03.020 --> 01:56:11.820
 Yeah, and I don't fully understand all the details, but in my conversations with our legislators, here's my interpretation.

01:56:11.820 --> 01:56:17.700
 It might not be 100% accurate, but Rocky Mountain Power has more or less a monopoly.

01:56:17.700 --> 01:56:24.520
 But then they have some restrictions on, some federal restrictions on them that are tight.

01:56:24.520 --> 01:56:37.520
 So, so they need, this is the way our, our legislators explained it, uh, in a very short time, is that, uh, there, there needs to be a split at Rocky Mountain Power.

01:56:37.520 --> 01:56:59.560
 That, that's what they expressed, uh, a path forward, but that's just Rocky Mountain Power, but some of it is, too, um, we're going to need, uh, both the federal and state government lined up to allow, to clear the path for these other, the regulatory stuff, to, to, to clear that path for power generation.

01:56:59.560 --> 01:57:15.820
 So, so we really need our federal delegation and our state delegation to line up, to be sitting in the same room with, and it's honestly, we're going to be sitting in there because it all surrounds power for the data centers that exist in, in our city.

01:57:16.100 --> 01:57:21.600
 I don't know if there's any other place that would be time to move that, if there are, they should be in that same room as well.

01:57:21.600 --> 01:57:22.600
 And Burgess has.

01:57:22.600 --> 01:57:24.600
 I was just going to say, is Burgess invited?

01:57:24.600 --> 01:57:36.600
 We have been invited to some of those meetings, so I know he's, he's been there, but I think, um, I'm just saying, Congressman Curtis is effective.

01:57:36.600 --> 01:57:38.600
 Well, that's what I'm saying, he should be there.

01:57:38.600 --> 01:57:39.100
 Yeah.

01:57:39.100 --> 01:57:44.600
 Any of our elected federal officials, we need to have a meeting tomorrow, they've been invited, I mean, that's.

01:57:44.600 --> 01:57:46.600
 We're a little ahead.

01:57:46.600 --> 01:57:48.600
 We have not been able to get them here yet.

01:57:48.600 --> 01:57:51.600
 Of course, they just got out of elections.

01:57:51.600 --> 01:57:55.600
 We need to set a date for it where you can tell them this is something.

01:57:55.600 --> 01:58:05.600
 Well, as I've been, staff can attest to it, I've been telling them, I might not be around much, because I need to, like, put all my time into this.

01:58:05.600 --> 01:58:07.600
 You're probably right.

01:58:07.600 --> 01:58:14.600
 There's probably no more pressing thing to be looking outward than this right now, because it's our economic engine.

01:58:14.600 --> 01:58:15.600
 All right.

01:58:15.600 --> 01:58:16.600
 Yeah.

01:58:16.600 --> 01:58:17.600
 Cities.

01:58:17.600 --> 01:58:18.600
 Based on.

01:58:18.600 --> 01:58:19.600
 It totally is.

01:58:19.600 --> 01:58:20.600
 Totally is.

01:58:20.600 --> 01:58:23.600
 And you were saying, like you were saying, a lot of stuff stacked up on that, like the school district.

01:58:23.600 --> 01:58:24.600
 Yeah.

01:58:24.600 --> 01:58:25.600
 I mean, that's how we're going to pay for this school.

01:58:25.600 --> 01:58:26.600
 Yeah.

01:58:26.600 --> 01:58:27.600
 Exactly.

01:58:27.600 --> 01:58:29.600
 I can tell people we're getting swimming pools, not what I'm doing.

01:58:29.600 --> 01:58:33.600
 I just have one quick question from the council so we can get some direction on it.

01:58:33.600 --> 01:58:36.600
 We've got an alternative energy code we're working on.

01:58:36.600 --> 01:58:45.600
 It's addressing, like, gas-powered power plants, small modular reactors, and solar.

01:58:45.600 --> 01:58:49.600
 Do you want us to address the full-scale nuclear facility?

01:58:49.600 --> 01:58:50.600
 Yes.

01:58:50.600 --> 01:58:51.600
 Any and all.

01:58:51.600 --> 01:58:53.600
 We want any and all power.

01:58:53.600 --> 01:58:56.600
 The reality of it is we need to look at all of it.

01:58:56.600 --> 01:59:01.600
 And what we don't want to do is we don't want to say yes to something that would have

01:59:01.600 --> 01:59:04.600
 a negative impact on doing something that would be more beneficial.

01:59:04.600 --> 01:59:09.600
 So what I'm saying is if all the land gets taken up with solar, for instance, and that's

01:59:09.600 --> 01:59:13.600
 the land that needs to have the nuclear facility on it, we shot ourselves in the foot.

01:59:13.600 --> 01:59:14.600
 Yeah.

01:59:14.600 --> 01:59:16.600
 Because the output's not even close to the same.

01:59:16.600 --> 01:59:20.600
 So that's why I think these conversations, it's not something that can be, you know, waiting

01:59:20.600 --> 01:59:21.600
 for spring.

01:59:21.600 --> 01:59:24.600
 Like, we need to jump on this as soon as possible.

01:59:24.600 --> 01:59:29.600
 And that means that all of us have got to become really smart about this subject fast.

01:59:29.600 --> 01:59:33.600
 So we need to understand the small, what were they called?

01:59:33.600 --> 01:59:34.600
 The small modular reactors.

01:59:34.600 --> 01:59:35.600
 Small modular reactors.

01:59:35.600 --> 01:59:39.600
 We need to be smart about what the output limitations of that and what the negatives of

01:59:39.600 --> 01:59:40.600
 that is.

01:59:40.600 --> 01:59:45.600
 The same thing with solar and with gas powered, natural gas powered generators.

01:59:45.600 --> 01:59:46.600
 Generators.

01:59:46.600 --> 01:59:52.600
 So we need to know these things and then evaluate and say, are we getting what we need out of this?

01:59:52.600 --> 01:59:56.600
 And if it really is that we need to go to a full nuclear plant, what are the downsides

01:59:56.600 --> 01:59:57.600
 of that?

01:59:57.600 --> 01:59:59.600
 Do we want that sitting in our valley?

01:59:59.600 --> 02:00:02.600
 Or do we need to work with some communities to the south that are not going to be growing

02:00:02.600 --> 02:00:06.600
 for another, you know, 75 years to work some of those things out?

02:00:06.600 --> 02:00:12.600
 But if we wait, everybody else makes the decision for us and then we're just the recipient of

02:00:12.600 --> 02:00:13.600
 their decisions.

02:00:13.600 --> 02:00:23.600
 And Evan, if I'm off on this, correct me, but we, our intention is to have a plan in place

02:00:23.600 --> 02:00:24.600
 by first quarter.

02:00:24.600 --> 02:00:25.600
 A power plant?

02:00:25.600 --> 02:00:26.600
 A plan.

02:00:26.600 --> 02:00:27.600
 For what?

02:00:27.600 --> 02:00:28.600
 For moving forward the power generation.

02:00:28.600 --> 02:00:29.600
 Yeah.

02:00:29.600 --> 02:00:30.600
 That's right.

02:00:30.600 --> 02:00:42.600
 So we have something that's articulable that we can share with those players that we want

02:00:42.600 --> 02:00:43.600
 to continue to grow.

02:00:43.600 --> 02:00:47.600
 I think the thing is that we're working with them.

02:00:47.600 --> 02:00:48.600
 Yeah.

02:00:48.600 --> 02:00:49.600
 Yes.

02:00:49.600 --> 02:00:54.600
 What kind of what may are saying too, is those players are already talking to Rocky Mountain

02:00:54.600 --> 02:00:56.600
 Power and the state and all of those other things.

02:00:56.600 --> 02:01:00.600
 Yeah, but the problem is Rocky Mountain is telling them, sorry, nothing we can do and just turn

02:01:00.600 --> 02:01:01.600
 on their hat.

02:01:01.600 --> 02:01:10.600
 So on Monday, I was invited to accompany a group of legislators with all of the head people

02:01:10.600 --> 02:01:16.600
 at Meta to talk about the power and what the chamber is doing for small business and what

02:01:16.600 --> 02:01:19.600
 Meta has given back to the city, like the community.

02:01:19.600 --> 02:01:22.600
 And the legislators aren't ours.

02:01:22.600 --> 02:01:27.600
 They were ones from all over the state that came down because that's their particular interest.

02:01:27.600 --> 02:01:32.600
 So those conversations they're having and they were having them completely outside of

02:01:32.600 --> 02:01:37.600
 us because I was only there because I run the chamber, not because I was a sneak outspenter.

02:01:37.600 --> 02:01:44.600
 Do we know who the committee members are over at the state level?

02:01:44.600 --> 02:01:45.600
 At the state level?

02:01:45.600 --> 02:01:47.600
 We need to probably find that out, right?

02:01:47.600 --> 02:01:54.600
 So I can tell you the ones that were there and I can give you some of that information.

02:01:54.600 --> 02:02:00.600
 But yeah, like for example, you know, DAI and their lobbyists are heavily engaged.

02:02:00.600 --> 02:02:03.600
 Is our lobbyist engaged?

02:02:03.600 --> 02:02:04.600
 Yes.

02:02:04.600 --> 02:02:07.600
 Yeah, I would assume.

02:02:07.600 --> 02:02:10.600
 So, yeah, Evan has a comment.

02:02:10.600 --> 02:02:13.600
 I was going to say, there's two different things that you are going to have to be presented

02:02:13.600 --> 02:02:14.600
 with at some point.

02:02:14.600 --> 02:02:16.600
 I'm sure it's going to come up.

02:02:16.600 --> 02:02:20.600
 One is just how close you're comfortable with power generation being in the homes.

02:02:20.600 --> 02:02:25.600
 And then the other is one way around rock around power is to have new transmission lines

02:02:25.600 --> 02:02:28.600
 by power generation companies that are separate.

02:02:28.600 --> 02:02:31.600
 Transmission lines and potentially substations.

02:02:31.600 --> 02:02:34.600
 So are you okay with that?

02:02:34.600 --> 02:02:35.600
 Okay with what?

02:02:35.600 --> 02:02:36.600
 Meaning the distance.

02:02:36.600 --> 02:02:37.600
 And I think that's what Brandon's working on.

02:02:37.600 --> 02:02:39.600
 He's not in here, but that's part of that.

02:02:39.600 --> 02:02:40.600
 Right.

02:02:40.600 --> 02:02:45.600
 We need to know what are our limitations for those pros and cons of it, right?

02:02:45.600 --> 02:02:46.600
 What's the impact?

02:02:46.600 --> 02:02:47.600
 All those things.

02:02:47.600 --> 02:02:48.600
 Yeah.

02:02:48.600 --> 02:02:49.600
 But that's the question.

02:02:49.600 --> 02:02:53.600
 And I think for me to boil it down in its simplest terms is what is Eagle Mountain willing

02:02:53.600 --> 02:02:54.600
 to do?

02:02:54.600 --> 02:03:00.600
 What can Eagle Mountain do to help facilitate this power generation that we all agree we want

02:03:00.600 --> 02:03:01.600
 to come to the Valley?

02:03:01.600 --> 02:03:02.600
 At least in some form.

02:03:02.600 --> 02:03:03.600
 Right.

02:03:03.600 --> 02:03:04.600
 Without destroying them.

02:03:04.600 --> 02:03:05.600
 Yes.

02:03:05.600 --> 02:03:10.600
 And which forms of that generation are we going to find ourselves most comfortable with?

02:03:10.600 --> 02:03:13.600
 And which are we going to accept the risks for?

02:03:13.600 --> 02:03:14.600
 I mean that's the reality.

02:03:14.600 --> 02:03:17.600
 So we need to know the impact for each one of these things, right?

02:03:17.600 --> 02:03:23.600
 So transmission lines, what the power generation source is, how that impacts wildlife, how that

02:03:23.600 --> 02:03:27.600
 impacts humans, all that in proximity to houses.

02:03:27.600 --> 02:03:28.600
 I think all of that information is...

02:03:28.600 --> 02:03:31.600
 And just remember, we also impact our neighbors.

02:03:31.600 --> 02:03:32.600
 Yes.

02:03:32.600 --> 02:03:34.600
 If we say, just put it at the south end of the city.

02:03:34.600 --> 02:03:38.600
 Well, and that's part of why I think Councilmember Gray.

02:03:38.600 --> 02:03:41.600
 I think that's part of why I think gathering up that data.

02:03:41.600 --> 02:03:45.600
 Because look, if we're all going to say no to whatever comes, then there's no sense in

02:03:45.600 --> 02:03:46.600
 even having a conversation.

02:03:46.600 --> 02:03:47.600
 Yeah.

02:03:47.600 --> 02:03:53.600
 But if we as a city can get to a place that says, this looks like this might be acceptable,

02:03:53.600 --> 02:03:57.600
 and these are the things that we think are most important, then we can go back and have

02:03:57.600 --> 02:04:02.600
 little conversations with the power generation companies that are perhaps off Rocky Mountain

02:04:02.600 --> 02:04:03.600
 Power's lines.

02:04:03.600 --> 02:04:04.600
 Because that's a major deal.

02:04:04.600 --> 02:04:05.600
 They own the infrastructure.

02:04:05.600 --> 02:04:06.600
 Yeah.

02:04:06.600 --> 02:04:07.600
 The delivery system.

02:04:07.600 --> 02:04:10.600
 So unless you're going to bring in a new delivery system, someone's going to pay

02:04:10.600 --> 02:04:15.600
 for it, you're going to have a hard time getting it where you need it to go anyway.

02:04:15.600 --> 02:04:22.600
 So I'm hearing that you would like Evan and Economic Development to put together some information

02:04:22.600 --> 02:04:23.600
 for you.

02:04:23.600 --> 02:04:24.600
 Whatever we have.

02:04:24.600 --> 02:04:27.600
 Now, some of that we're still trying to gather from these businesses.

02:04:27.600 --> 02:04:30.600
 For instance, the small natural gas facilities.

02:04:30.600 --> 02:04:32.600
 Those are the very questions we ask them.

02:04:32.600 --> 02:04:37.600
 We need the data so that we can determine how close these can be to residents and others.

02:04:37.600 --> 02:04:38.600
 They don't have that yet.

02:04:38.600 --> 02:04:41.600
 But whatever we can gather, we'll provide that.

02:04:41.600 --> 02:04:44.600
 What you'll find, Steve, is just like what we did with the school district.

02:04:44.600 --> 02:04:48.600
 I didn't know hardly anything about the school district from that conversation that first

02:04:48.600 --> 02:04:49.600
 started taking place.

02:04:49.600 --> 02:04:50.600
 But we'll dig in.

02:04:50.600 --> 02:04:51.600
 We'll learn.

02:04:51.600 --> 02:04:52.600
 We'll research.

02:04:52.600 --> 02:04:54.600
 And then we'll learn with you.

02:04:54.600 --> 02:04:55.600
 And together, we'll figure out--

02:04:55.600 --> 02:04:57.600
 So if we're all sharing information and--

02:04:57.600 --> 02:04:58.600
 Yeah.

02:04:58.600 --> 02:05:02.600
 Well, and then I think once we get on the same page and we've kind of determined what's

02:05:02.600 --> 02:05:09.600
 our threshold and what we can handle, we do need to start some sort of PSAs or something

02:05:09.600 --> 02:05:11.600
 to help the community better understand.

02:05:11.600 --> 02:05:15.600
 So if we were to go nuclear, if we were to-- the different pieces.

02:05:15.600 --> 02:05:21.600
 So-- because outside entities always come in when there's a power generation thing in the

02:05:21.600 --> 02:05:25.600
 city and they skew what the results and choices.

02:05:25.600 --> 02:05:30.600
 So if we can be ahead of that so that we can share that info and educate, it's going to

02:05:30.600 --> 02:05:32.600
 be a way better outcome.

02:05:32.600 --> 02:05:39.600
 Well, I think people will be aware that when people hear nuclear, they immediately think

02:05:39.600 --> 02:05:40.600
 of the Japan facility.

02:05:40.600 --> 02:05:41.600
 Yeah.

02:05:41.600 --> 02:05:46.600
 If we're talking about meltdowns and people going third armies, everybody worries when

02:05:46.600 --> 02:05:50.600
 they hear nuclear generated power, which is such a rare problem.

02:05:50.600 --> 02:05:55.600
 And a lot of those things that happened, you know, the Japan one was a tsunami that, you

02:05:55.600 --> 02:05:57.600
 know, how could they prepare for that?

02:05:57.600 --> 02:06:00.600
 Some of the other ones were just mismanagement of old technology.

02:06:00.600 --> 02:06:01.600
 Well, and it--

02:06:01.600 --> 02:06:08.600
 It's come a long way since then, and they're even bringing back most of those nuclear plants

02:06:08.600 --> 02:06:13.600
 that were economically shut down, not that they had any problems with safety.

02:06:13.600 --> 02:06:15.600
 And that's countrywide right now.

02:06:15.600 --> 02:06:17.600
 So it's something that's already at the forefront.

02:06:17.600 --> 02:06:23.600
 I just think that we need to be in the conversation because it directly impacts us economically.

02:06:23.600 --> 02:06:29.600
 But perhaps, to Darren's point, it might be another opportunity for a revenue now, creating

02:06:29.600 --> 02:06:32.600
 a source for you now.

02:06:32.600 --> 02:06:38.600
 You know, one of the questions that comes up for me is, do we need to rethink our annexation

02:06:38.600 --> 02:06:40.600
 policy southward?

02:06:40.600 --> 02:06:41.600
 No question.

02:06:41.600 --> 02:06:46.600
 So, okay, since you brought it up, I was starting to decide when to bring this in.

02:06:46.600 --> 02:06:48.600
 That's a good transition into growth and housing update.

02:06:48.600 --> 02:06:49.600
 Yeah.

02:06:49.600 --> 02:06:56.600
 So there's going to be a lot of goals and objectives that are going to have, you know,

02:06:56.600 --> 02:06:57.600
 overlap, right?

02:06:57.600 --> 02:07:03.600
 So, for example, to expand on, you know, local power generation, annexation might be part

02:07:03.600 --> 02:07:04.600
 of that.

02:07:04.600 --> 02:07:10.600
 To expand on outdoor recreation and events, annexation is going to be part of that.

02:07:10.600 --> 02:07:21.600
 So, one of the things I want to follow up with each of you on, I'll bounce this off of staff.

02:07:21.600 --> 02:07:29.600
 I'm considering, what I'd like to do is create, I'm not sure what to call them, action committees,

02:07:29.600 --> 02:07:36.600
 steering committees, that are focused on, focused on these, that need a little extra attention.

02:07:36.600 --> 02:07:44.600
 Something like annexation to really, to create these, to create a small, dynamic committee

02:07:44.600 --> 02:07:48.600
 that can focus on that and help them move forward.

02:07:48.600 --> 02:07:49.600
 Like a work group?

02:07:49.600 --> 02:07:50.600
 Committees.

02:07:50.600 --> 02:07:51.600
 Yeah.

02:07:51.600 --> 02:07:52.600
 The reality is, I'm in favor of that.

02:07:52.600 --> 02:07:57.600
 I've said the committee idea to put all of the right minds over each of these scenes,

02:07:57.600 --> 02:07:58.600
 because we can't all be everywhere.

02:07:58.600 --> 02:07:59.600
 Right.

02:07:59.600 --> 02:08:00.600
 You can't.

02:08:00.600 --> 02:08:01.600
 And you can't study everything.

02:08:01.600 --> 02:08:06.600
 And the staff, they can't spend all their time on one particular thing all the time.

02:08:06.600 --> 02:08:11.600
 So, if we have these moments where we can gather information, bring that information back

02:08:11.600 --> 02:08:15.600
 to the council, and say, okay, this is what we learned from this session of the committee.

02:08:15.600 --> 02:08:16.600
 I think it's a great idea.

02:08:16.600 --> 02:08:19.600
 It's what is done at every other level of government.

02:08:19.600 --> 02:08:21.600
 We should be doing the same thing.

02:08:21.600 --> 02:08:28.600
 So, that's what, you know, I've got a few ideas of objectives that maybe ought to be considered

02:08:28.600 --> 02:08:35.600
 for a committee, but feel free to share if you've got ideas of things.

02:08:35.600 --> 02:08:37.600
 I think we've identified a few of them today.

02:08:37.600 --> 02:08:38.600
 Yeah.

02:08:38.600 --> 02:08:42.600
 There needs to be something that deals with our open space and parks and how we deal with

02:08:42.600 --> 02:08:43.600
 that.

02:08:43.600 --> 02:08:44.600
 Yeah.

02:08:44.600 --> 02:08:50.600
 Public works, so basically our utilities that we've provided already, having something

02:08:50.600 --> 02:08:54.600
 to help how we're going to move forward with some of those things.

02:08:54.600 --> 02:08:59.600
 Power generation and data centers obviously is going to have a big pull on that.

02:08:59.600 --> 02:09:04.600
 So, I think whatever your list is, send it out to us and let's spend some time in our

02:09:04.600 --> 02:09:05.600
 next city council meeting.

02:09:05.600 --> 02:09:09.600
 Maybe we take 30 minutes and say, okay, what are some committees that would be meaningful

02:09:09.600 --> 02:09:12.600
 and what's the, when are we going to implement them?

02:09:12.600 --> 02:09:14.600
 Just a January thing?

02:09:14.600 --> 02:09:19.600
 Like, it gives us time to be able to kind of form them and then say, okay, let's go.

02:09:19.600 --> 02:09:24.600
 Maybe we could unofficially get them all even before then probably, but I suspect we'll

02:09:24.600 --> 02:09:27.600
 probably get a lot of traction until January.

02:09:27.600 --> 02:09:31.600
 Well, could we just have a discussion tomorrow in our extra time that we have?

02:09:31.600 --> 02:09:32.600
 Why not?

02:09:32.600 --> 02:09:36.600
 And just say, okay, here's some of the ones we're thinking of and then kind of hash that

02:09:36.600 --> 02:09:39.600
 out and then revisit it in that council meeting soon.

02:09:39.600 --> 02:09:44.600
 Mayor, could you send us your ideas so that we can kind of rumerate on that tonight?

02:09:44.600 --> 02:09:47.600
 And then we can come back tomorrow during our time and have a discussion about it?

02:09:47.600 --> 02:09:48.600
 Sure.

02:09:48.600 --> 02:09:49.600
 Thank you.

02:09:49.600 --> 02:09:50.600
 Okay.

02:09:50.600 --> 02:09:51.600
 Okay.

02:09:51.600 --> 02:09:53.600
 We're getting bingo time there.

02:09:53.600 --> 02:09:55.600
 Any questions on the engineering list?

02:09:55.600 --> 02:09:56.600
 No problem.

02:09:56.600 --> 02:09:59.600
 Hey, I'm just glad that we have our engineering department.

02:09:59.600 --> 02:10:02.600
 So with staff, then go to Mary.

02:10:02.600 --> 02:10:05.600
 Well, I wanted to say one thing before we jump in.

02:10:05.600 --> 02:10:13.600
 Just FYI, Vince is hard at work now on getting all of our master plans updated for utilities.

02:10:13.600 --> 02:10:22.600
 So water, sewer, storm drain, transportation, we have the parks one going as well.

